WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.679
This is the Reason Interview with

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:02.680
Nick Gillespie.

00:00:07.910 --> 00:00:09.071
All right. Thanks for coming out,

00:00:09.072 --> 00:00:10.849
this is part of Reason's Little

00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:12.679
Singer series of

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:13.579
salons.

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:15.259
And our guest tonight is James

00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:17.179
Kirchick, the author

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:18.769
most recently of the book "Secret

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:20.779
City: The Hidden History of Gay

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:22.339
Washington." We're going to be

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:24.859
talking to him about his work,

00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:26.569
uh, and his career and his

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:27.859
commitments to free speech.

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:30.289
You define yourself Jamie, uh,

00:00:30.290 --> 00:00:31.189
which is how you go by.

00:00:31.190 --> 00:00:32.899
You define yourself as a free speech

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:34.519
absolutist.

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:34.969
Yeah.

00:00:34.970 --> 00:00:35.779
Yeah.

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:37.649
Um, why don't we start by tell

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:39.649
me how, what does that mean?

00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:41.719
Like, because you obviously

00:00:41.720 --> 00:00:43.580
don't believe in free speech

00:00:43.581 --> 00:00:45.649
always being

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:47.839
legal, do you?

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:49.579
I support the First Amendment of the

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:50.599
United States Constitution.

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:51.289
I've never heard of that.

00:00:51.290 --> 00:00:53.239
Which is, uh, the most

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:55.129
expansive in terms of

00:00:55.130 --> 00:00:57.559
any law of any country in the world

00:00:57.560 --> 00:00:59.449
and basically permits any speech

00:00:59.450 --> 00:01:01.819
that is short of

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:03.709
inciting imminent violence.

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:06.019
Or it could be or harassment.

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:06.379
Right?

00:01:06.380 --> 00:01:08.299
Yeah. Short of that, um,

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:10.219
I think we should have a pretty wide

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:11.209
berth.

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:13.339
Uh, you, uh, recently turned 40

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:14.209
years old.

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:15.709
So you are, what, a millennial.

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:16.819
Technically, I turned 40.

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:17.929
Yes.

00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:19.759
Uh, on the inside, you're obviously

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:20.339
much older.

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:20.839
Yeah.

00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:22.969
Um. But why?

00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:24.979
I mean, so millennials and even

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:26.809
worse, Gen Z, are there any Gen Z

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:28.279
in the audience?

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:29.569
Okay, look at one.

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:31.699
Uh, um, but,

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:33.799
um, why are you a free speech

00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:35.569
absolutist? Your generation does not

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:37.040
believe in free speech anymore.

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:40.459
I mean, I, I maybe trace it back

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:41.479
to, I think when I was in sixth

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:43.459
grade and I read "1984"

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:45.859
and I wasn't really politically

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:47.359
aware of anything or much

00:01:48.650 --> 00:01:50.389
until that point.

00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:52.099
Um, and that book just sort of

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:53.299
opened up a world to me.

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:54.499
It sort of it taught me what the

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:55.879
Cold War was.

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:57.139
Uh, it was great literature.

00:01:57.140 --> 00:01:58.999
It taught me what freedom meant to

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:01.639
taught me what authoritarianism was.

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:03.829
I think the next year I joined

00:02:03.830 --> 00:02:05.539
Amnesty International, which was a

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:07.279
very different organization.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:08.929
We're talking like almost 30 years

00:02:08.930 --> 00:02:10.309
ago, was a very different

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:11.539
organization. It was mostly

00:02:11.540 --> 00:02:12.540
concerned with...You

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:16.159
write letters to dictatorships

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:17.959
to free political prisoners.

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:20.599
So every week could be some new,

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:22.519
you know, uh, person

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:24.079
in some benighted land.

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:25.189
And it was literally you'd write

00:02:25.190 --> 00:02:26.509
like, you know, "Dear, uh,

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:28.759
Comandante, uh, you know,

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:30.319
Juarez" or something like, 'Please

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:32.239
release this writer from

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:33.739
jail." Um,

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:36.769
and then I also actually

00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:38.419
think, I think being a closeted gay

00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:40.249
person from, you know, until I was

00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:40.329
18.

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:40.374
I think your secret's out.

00:02:40.375 --> 00:02:42.499
Not anymore.

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:44.269
Not anymore. But I think being in

00:02:44.270 --> 00:02:45.559
the closet for, you know, until I

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:47.539
was 18, um,

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:49.459
taught me the value of

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:52.129
expression and honesty and

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:54.109
and speaking your truth.

00:02:54.110 --> 00:02:55.190
I hate that term, but,

00:02:56.510 --> 00:02:58.789
uh, well, because it's become

00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:01.369
a sort of form of relativism.

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:02.479
You know my truth.

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:03.529
There is a truth, right?

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:05.689
But anyway, I think being,

00:03:05.690 --> 00:03:07.969
um, uh, understanding

00:03:07.970 --> 00:03:09.679
what it means to have to lie about

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:10.849
yourself, about something so

00:03:10.850 --> 00:03:12.679
integral to who you are, it

00:03:12.680 --> 00:03:14.779
just made me very sensitive to this

00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:15.559
issue.

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:16.999
I mean, in my book "Secret City," I

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:19.039
compare sort of the plight

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:21.049
of gay men and women in America 50,

00:03:21.050 --> 00:03:23.209
60 years ago to that of dissidents

00:03:23.210 --> 00:03:24.949
in a communist country.

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:26.779
Because their

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:28.399
bars were raided by the police,

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:30.139
their phones were tapped by the FBI.

00:03:30.140 --> 00:03:30.979
They had to live a lie.

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:32.179
They had to pretend to be something

00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:33.199
that they weren't.

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:34.609
And so I really do think that being

00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:36.709
gay and sort of, you know, suffering

00:03:36.710 --> 00:03:37.819
in the closet in high school and

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:39.739
what not made me much more,

00:03:39.740 --> 00:03:41.689
uh, supportive of free speech.

00:03:41.690 --> 00:03:43.759
Um, I guess a couple things

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:44.899
while you're talking.

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:46.849
Um, I realize I have a lot

00:03:46.850 --> 00:03:48.619
of bills that I need to pay.

00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:50.449
Um, you know, other things in my

00:03:50.450 --> 00:03:51.309
mind came up.

00:03:51.310 --> 00:03:54.139
That was a joke that obviously

00:03:54.140 --> 00:03:55.729
did not go over right.

00:03:55.730 --> 00:03:57.469
You should host the Golden Globes.

00:03:57.470 --> 00:03:58.129
Have you thought about that?

00:03:58.130 --> 00:04:00.019
No, I turn that down for this, if

00:04:00.020 --> 00:04:00.809
you can imagine.

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:01.729
Right.

00:04:01.730 --> 00:04:03.619
Um. But, um.

00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:04.759
What?

00:04:04.760 --> 00:04:06.799
Why was it difficult to come out?

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:08.879
And what made it easy?

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:10.209
There...not easy, but what?

00:04:10.210 --> 00:04:11.659
Well, I first of all, everything's

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:12.439
relative.

00:04:12.440 --> 00:04:14.479
And, uh, you know, I, I

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:16.338
grew up in a very,

00:04:16.339 --> 00:04:18.049
you know, pretty liberal, accepting

00:04:18.050 --> 00:04:19.489
household. I was very lucky in that

00:04:19.490 --> 00:04:20.299
sense.

00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:22.369
I went to an all boys school, um,

00:04:22.370 --> 00:04:23.569
which, you know, all male

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:25.789
environments tend to be more.

00:04:25.790 --> 00:04:26.869
Homophobic.

00:04:26.870 --> 00:04:27.085
And homophilic in a sense right.

00:04:27.086 --> 00:04:28.039
Because it's all affiliated.

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:29.879
Yeah. My high school, unfortunately,

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:31.759
it was not it it was, uh,

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:33.529
um, but, you know, it's a kind of,

00:04:33.530 --> 00:04:34.759
you know, bravado and, you know,

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:36.379
proving you're not gay is very much

00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:37.759
a part of those kinds of

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:39.229
environments. I think a lot, by the

00:04:39.230 --> 00:04:41.149
way, has changed in the

00:04:41.150 --> 00:04:42.709
22 years since.

00:04:42.710 --> 00:04:44.689
I mean, everyone's coming out now

00:04:44.690 --> 00:04:46.489
at 12 or 13 it seems like.

00:04:46.490 --> 00:04:48.619
Half, half of Gen Z is LGBT.

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:49.819
I don't believe that for a second,

00:04:49.820 --> 00:04:50.719
by the way.

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:51.679
But that's what it says according to

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:52.819
the polls. It's actually, it's

00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:55.099
incredible when you think of the

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:57.139
the dramatic change where being

00:04:57.140 --> 00:04:59.239
gay or LGBT, queer,

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:00.799
whatever term you want to use, was

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:03.349
considered a bad thing,

00:05:03.350 --> 00:05:04.880
a terrible thing, a sin and what not.

